<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The dichromatic number <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> of a digraph <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=(V,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=(V,A)</annotation></semantics></math> is the minimum number of sets in a partition <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},\ldots{},V_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},\ldots{},V_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> subsets so that the induced subdigraph <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[V_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[V_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic for each <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a generalization of the chromatic number for undirected graphs as a graph has chromatic number at most <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the complete biorientation 
<span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_markedasmath ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="abstract1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="abstract1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">↔</span></span>
<span id="abstract1.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="abstract1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><math id="abstract1.m9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hfil\textstyle G\hfil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hfil\textstyle G\hfil</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span> of <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> (replace each edge by a directed 2-cycle) has dichromatic number at most <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper we introduce the acyclic dichromatic number <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> of a digraph <math id="abstract1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> as the minimum number of sets in a partition <math id="abstract1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},\ldots{},V_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},\ldots{},V_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="abstract1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="abstract1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> subsets so that the induced subdigraph <math id="abstract1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[V_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[V_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic for each <math id="abstract1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math> and each of the bipartite induced subdigraphs <math id="abstract1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[V_{i},V_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[V_{i},V_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic for each <math id="abstract1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>. This parameter, which resembles the definition of acyclic chromatic number for undirected graphs, has apparently not been studied before.
We derive a number of results which display the difference between the dichromatic number and the acyclic dichromatic number, in particular, there are digraphs <math id="abstract1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with arbitrarily large <math id="abstract1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{}(D)-\vec{\chi}{}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{}(D)-\vec{\chi}{}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>, even among tournaments with dichromatic number 2 and bipartite tournaments (where the dichromatic number is always 2). We prove several complexity results, including that deciding whether <math id="abstract1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{}(D)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{}(D)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> is NP-complete already for bipartite digraphs, while it is polynomial for tournaments (contrary to the case for dichromatic number).
We also generalize the concept of heroes of a tournament to acyclic heroes of tournaments.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords:</span> Dichromatic number; acyclic dichromatic number; tournaments; bipartite tournaments; split digraphs; NP-completeness; acyclic heroes.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Terminology not provided below is consistent with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given an integer <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, a <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-colouring</span> of a digraph <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a mapping <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon V(D)\to[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon V(D)\to[k]</annotation></semantics></math>. The sets <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},\dots,V_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},\dots,V_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}=\varphi^{-1}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}=\varphi^{-1}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> are called the <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">colour classes</span> of <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say that <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-dicolouring</span> if, for every <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[V_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[V_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic. The <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dichromatic number</span> of <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the least integer <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> admits an acyclic <math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring.
The notions of dicolouring and dichromatic number were introduced by Erdős and Neumann-Lara in the late 1970s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, rediscovered independently by Mohar <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> in the 2000s, and gained a lot of attention since then. It turned out that many classical results on graph colouring admit a generalisation to the directed setting involving the dichromatic number, see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce the notion of <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acyclic dicolouring</span> of oriented graphs (that is, digraphs with at most one arc between any two vertices). A <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, with colour classes <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},\dots,V_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},\dots,V_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, is <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acyclic</span> if, for every <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>, the bipartite oriented graph <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[V_{i},V_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[V_{i},V_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic.
The <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acyclic dichromatic number</span> of <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>, is the least integer <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> admits a acyclic <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring. Note that <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined, as every oriented graph <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> admits a trivial acyclic <math id="S1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(D)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(D)|</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This notion is a directed analogue of the one of <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acyclic colouring</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, which is a colouring of an undirected graph such that any two adjacent vertices receive distinct colours, and the union of any two colour classes induces a forest (<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> is acyclic). In particular, if <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is an oriented graph with underlying graph <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, then any acyclic colouring of <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> yields an acyclic dicolouring of <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and in particular <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant\chi_{\rm a}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant\chi_{\rm a}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{\rm a}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{\rm a}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the acyclic chromatic number of <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Adding the restriction that also the bipartite subdigraphs <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[V_{i},V_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[V_{i},V_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> are acyclic for each <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math> may force us to use (many) more colours in an acyclic dicolouring of a digraph <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> than if we just require each <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[V_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[V_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> to be acyclic. This also has the effect that the complexity of deciding whether <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(D)}\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(D)}\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math> may differ from that of deciding whether <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}(D)\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}(D)\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>, namely, the first problem may be polynomial for some <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> even if the second one is NP-complete. Indeed, as a prominent case, we prove that this is the case for tournaments when <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is organized as follows. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 A construction of oriented graphs with large acyclic dichromatic number ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we show how to construct (bipartite) digraphs with arbitrarily high acyclic dichromatic number, showing that the difference between the acyclic dichromatic number and the dichromatic number can be arbitrarily high even for bipartite digraphs. We use this to show that the same holds also for the class of tournaments of dichromatic number 2. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we generalize the concept of heroes of tournaments to what we call acyclic heroes for tournaments, that is, tournaments so that every tournament that does not contain one of these as a subtournament has bounded acyclic dichromatic number. We formulate a conjecture describing which tournaments are exactly the acyclic heroes, and provide several results which support this conjecture. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we first use the construction from Section <a href="#S2" title="2 A construction of oriented graphs with large acyclic dichromatic number ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> to obtain an easy proof that determining whether <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math> is NP-complete for every <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, even for bipartite digraphs. We also show that the same conclusion holds for split digraphs. We then show that, contrary (assuming P is not equal to NP) to the case for the dichromatic number, one can check in polynomial time whether a tournament has acyclic dichromatic number at most 2. In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we derive tight upper bounds for the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments and in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Degenerate graphs ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> we show that every 2-degenerate oriented graph has acyclic dichromatic number at most 2 while there are 3-degenerate oriented graphs with arbitrarily high acyclic dichromatic number. Finally, in Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Remarks and further open problems ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> we pose a number of open problems on acyclic dichromatic numbers of oriented graphs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>A construction of oriented graphs with large acyclic dichromatic number</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is well-known that the random tournament on <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices has dichromatic number <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(n/\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(n/\log n)</annotation></semantics></math> with high probability, see <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, Section 4]</cite>. In Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 24. ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a> below, we show that the random tournament on <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices has acyclic dichromatic number <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-\Theta(\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-\Theta(\log n)</annotation></semantics></math> with high probability, so there exist tournaments <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> whose gap between <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(1-o(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(1-o(1))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide a construction of tournaments with dichromatic number <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> but arbitrarily large acyclic dichromatic number that will be used later on.
For any undirected graph <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding symmetric digraph 
<span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_markedasmath ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.p2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">↔</span></span>
<span id="S2.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.p2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.p2.m3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hfil\textstyle G\hfil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hfil\textstyle G\hfil</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span> is obtained by replacing each edge of <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by a directed 2-cycle. We let <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math> be the oriented graph obtained from 
<span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_markedasmath ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S2.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.p2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">↔</span></span>
<span id="S2.p2.1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.p2.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.p2.m6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hfil\textstyle G\hfil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hfil\textstyle G\hfil</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span> by performing the operation called <span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">vertex splitting</span> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, page 134]</cite>, that is, each vertex <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by two vertices <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{in},v_{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{in},v_{out}</annotation></semantics></math> and the arc <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{in}v_{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{in}v_{out}</annotation></semantics></math> and every arc <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by the arc <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{out}v_{in}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{out}v_{in}</annotation></semantics></math>. The digraph <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is bipartite with bipartition <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{in},V_{out})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{in},V_{out})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{in}=\{v_{in}:v\in V\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{in}=\{v_{in}:v\in V\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{out}=\{v_{out}:v\in V\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{out}=\{v_{out}:v\in V\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the only arcs from <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{in}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{in}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{out}</annotation></semantics></math> is the perfect matching <math id="S2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{in}v_{out}:v\in V\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{in}v_{out}:v\in V\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Observe that every edge <math id="S2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the directed 4-cycle <math id="S2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{in},u_{out},v_{in},v_{out})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{in},u_{out},v_{in},v_{out})</annotation></semantics></math> and that the only induced cycles of <math id="S2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math> are 4-cycles of the form above. See Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 A construction of oriented graphs with large acyclic dichromatic number ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for an example.</p>
</div>
