<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It turns out that <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D(G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D(G))</annotation></semantics></math> grows with <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every graph <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D(G))=\left\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D(G))=\left\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D(G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D(G))</annotation></semantics></math>.
We first prove that <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>.
To see this, let <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon V(D(G))\rightarrow[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon V(D(G))\rightarrow[k]</annotation></semantics></math> be an acyclic dicolouring of <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and assign each vertex <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> the colour <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(v)=(\varphi(v_{in}),\varphi(v_{out}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(v)=(\varphi(v_{in}),\varphi(v_{out}))</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper colouring of <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, it suffices to see that if <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in E</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge of <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(u)=\psi(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(u)=\psi(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, then either all vertices of the 4-cycle <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{in},u_{out},v_{in},v_{out})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{in},u_{out},v_{in},v_{out})</annotation></semantics></math> received the same colour <math id="S2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> or the vertices <math id="S2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{in},v_{in}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{in},v_{in}</annotation></semantics></math> received one colour <math id="S2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{out},v_{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{out},v_{out}</annotation></semantics></math> received another colour <math id="S2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>. In the first case <math id="S2.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{-1}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{-1}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> is not acyclic and in the second case the 4-cycle <math id="S2.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{in},u_{out},v_{in},v_{out})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{in},u_{out},v_{in},v_{out})</annotation></semantics></math> alternates between the sets <math id="S2.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{-1}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{-1}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{-1}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{-1}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> and both cases contradict that <math id="S2.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic dicolouring of <math id="S2.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> uses <math id="S2.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> colours, this shows that <math id="S2.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant\sqrt{\chi(G)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant\sqrt{\chi(G)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and since <math id="S2.p4.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is an integer we have <math id="S2.p4.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us now show that <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leqslant\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leqslant\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. For this, let <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> be the smallest square number with <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geqslant\chi(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geqslant\chi(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, so we want to show that <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leqslant\sqrt{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩽</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leqslant\sqrt{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:V(G)\rightarrow[\ell]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:V(G)\rightarrow[\ell]</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring of <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
We take an arbitrary bijection <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> between the set <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots{},\ell\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots{},\ell\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the set <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(1,1),\ldots{},(1,\sqrt{\ell}),(2,1),\ldots{},(2,\sqrt{\ell}),\ldots{}(\sqrt{\ell},\sqrt{\ell})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msqrt><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(1,1),\ldots{},(1,\sqrt{\ell}),(2,1),\ldots{},(2,\sqrt{\ell}),\ldots{}(\sqrt{\ell},\sqrt{\ell})\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and assign the colours <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math> to the vertices <math id="S2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{in},v_{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{in},v_{out}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\psi(v))=(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\psi(v))=(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>.
To see that <math id="S2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic dicolouring of <math id="S2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, it suffices to consider induced directed cycles of <math id="S2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math> which, by the remark above, are all 4-cycles of the form <math id="S2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=(u_{in},u_{out},v_{in},v_{out})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=(u_{in},u_{out},v_{in},v_{out})</annotation></semantics></math> for some pair <math id="S2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, and such a cycle corresponds to the edge <math id="S2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper colouring of <math id="S2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows from the definition of <math id="S2.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> above that <math id="S2.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> cannot assign the same colour to all four vertices of <math id="S2.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, so each colour class is indeed acyclic. Suppose finally that <math id="S2.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> alternates between <math id="S2.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{-1}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{-1}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{-1}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{-1}(j)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the definition of <math id="S2.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> implies that we have <math id="S2.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(u)=\psi(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(u)=\psi(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting that <math id="S2.p5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper colouring of <math id="S2.p5.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It follows from <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 A construction of oriented graphs with large acyclic dichromatic number ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> that there exist bipartite tournaments (<span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> orientations of bipartite complete graphs) with arbitrarily high acyclic dichromatic number, and trivially all bipartite digraphs have dichromatic number at most 2. The same thing holds for tournaments by completing both parts of the partition into a transitive tournament. A <span id="S2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">transitive tournament</span> is an acyclic tournament, and the unique (up to isomorphism) transitive tournament of order <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TT_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TT_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every integer <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a tournament <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}{(T)=2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}{(T)=2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(T)}\geqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(T)}\geqslant k</annotation></semantics></math> and such that <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has a spanning acyclic bipartite subdigraph.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=(V_{1},V_{2},A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=(V_{1},V_{2},A)</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite tournament with <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(B)}\geqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(B)}\geqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>, which exists by <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 A construction of oriented graphs with large acyclic dichromatic number ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (take for instance <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(K_{k^{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(K_{k^{2}})</annotation></semantics></math>), and form the tournament <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> from two copies <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},B_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},B_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> with vertex sets <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1,1}\cup{}V_{1,2},V_{2,1}\cup{}V_{2,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1,1}\cup{}V_{1,2},V_{2,1}\cup{}V_{2,2}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively by adding the arcs of a transitive tournament inside each <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and all arcs from <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1,1}\cup{}V_{1,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1,1}\cup{}V_{1,2}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2,1}\cup{}V_{2,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2,1}\cup{}V_{2,2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The partition <math id="S2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}=V_{1,1}\cup{}V_{2,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}=V_{1,1}\cup{}V_{2,1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}=V_{2,1}\cup{}V_{2,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}=V_{2,1}\cup{}V_{2,2}</annotation></semantics></math> shows that <math id="S2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}(T)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}(T)=2</annotation></semantics></math> and the fact that <math id="S2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is a subdigraph of <math id="S2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> shows that <math id="S2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(T)}\geqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(T)}\geqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, <math id="S2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has a spanning acyclic bipartite tournament induced by the arcs between <math id="S2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1,1}\cup{}V_{1,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1,1}\cup{}V_{1,2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2,1}\cup{}V_{2,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2,1}\cup{}V_{2,2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">This shows that having dichromatic number 2 and a spanning acyclic bipartite subdigraph does not imply that the acyclic dichromatic number is bounded, even for tournaments. We pose the following problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What is the complexity of deciding for given numbers <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1},k_{2}\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1},k_{2}\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math> whether a tournament <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> contains a spanning acyclic bipartite tournament <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=(V_{1},V_{2},A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=(V_{1},V_{2},A)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V_{i}|\geqslant k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V_{i}|\geqslant k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">A number of problems concerning the existence of spanning bipartite subdigraphs have been considered in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Acyclic heroes of tournaments</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be two tournaments. We say that <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-free</span> if <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain any copy of <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> as a subtournament. The class of <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-free tournaments is denoted by <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{F}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{F}(H)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The tournament <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hero</span> if there exists a constant <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> such that every tournament <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathfrak{F}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathfrak{F}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> has dichromatic number at most <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Berger <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> obtained an exact characterisation of heroes, for which we first need a few specific definitions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1},H_{2},H_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1},H_{2},H_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be three tournaments. We denote by <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}\Rightarrow H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}\Rightarrow H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the tournament obtained from disjoint copies of <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by putting all the arcs from <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. We further denote by <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(H_{1},H_{2},H_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(H_{1},H_{2},H_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> the tournament obtained from disjoint copies of <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1},H_{2},H_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1},H_{2},H_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> by putting all the arcs from <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, from <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>, and from <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the sake of better readability, in the <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> operator, we write <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for the transitive tournament <math id="S3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TT_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TT_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, so for instance <math id="S3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(H,1,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(H,1,k)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes <math id="S3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(H,TT_{1},TT_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(H,TT_{1},TT_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We let <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> be the smallest class of tournaments such that:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.i1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varnothing,TT_{1}\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varnothing,TT_{1}\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1},H_{2}\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1},H_{2}\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, the tournament <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}\Rightarrow H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}\Rightarrow H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.i3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>, both tournaments <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(H,k,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(H,k,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(H,1,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(H,1,k)</annotation></semantics></math> belong to <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It turns out that <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the class of heroes.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Berger <span id="Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A tournament <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a hero if and only if <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, a tournament <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is an <span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acyclic hero</span> if there exists an absolute constant <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> such that every tournament <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathfrak{F}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathfrak{F}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> has acyclic dichromatic number at most <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant\vec{\chi}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant\vec{\chi}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> holds for every tournament <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, observe that, if <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic hero, then it is also a hero, but the converse does not hold in general.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For every <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the tournament <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TT_{k}\Rightarrow(\Delta(k,1,1)\Rightarrow TT_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TT_{k}\Rightarrow(\Delta(k,1,1)\Rightarrow TT_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{AH}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{AH}</annotation></semantics></math> be the class of all tournaments appearing as a subtournament of <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for some integer <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. We pose the following conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A tournament <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic hero if and only if <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathcal{AH}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathcal{AH}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As evidences for the conjecture, we prove the following partial results in the next subsections.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic hero, then <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathcal{AH}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathcal{AH}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The tournament <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic hero.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every integer <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(k,1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(k,1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic hero.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">With Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> in hands, the following is a natural direction towards the resolution of Conjecture <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Conjecture 5. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Harutyunyan, Le, Thomassé, and Wu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> proved the following “local to global” property for tournaments.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Harutyunyan <span id="Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a function <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that every tournament <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}(T)\leqslant\max_{v\in V(T)}f\Bigg(\vec{\chi}\Big(T[N^{+}(v)]\Big)\Bigg)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}(T)\leqslant\max_{v\in V(T)}f\Bigg(\vec{\chi}\Big(T[N^{+}(v)]\Big)\Bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We believe that the same property holds for the acyclic dichromatic number, and pose the following conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a function <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that every tournament <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant\max_{v\in V(T)}f\Bigg(\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}\Big(T[N^{+}(v)]\Big)\Bigg)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant\max_{v\in V(T)}f\Bigg(\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}\Big(T[N^{+}(v)]\Big)\Bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">An easy inductive argument shows that, if true, Conjecture <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Conjecture 10. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> together with Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> imply Conjecture <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Conjecture 5. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a></h3>

<div id="Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 11</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The tournaments <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{C}_{3}\Rightarrow\vec{C}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{C}_{3}\Rightarrow\vec{C}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\vec{C_{3}},1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\vec{C_{3}},1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(2,2,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(2,2,1)</annotation></semantics></math> are not acyclic heroes.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us fix an arbitrary integer <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and exhibit a specific tournament <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant k</annotation></semantics></math> that does not contain any of the aforementioned tournaments.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be the tournament obtained as follows. We start from two transitive tournaments on <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> vertices with acyclic orderings <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1},\dots,u_{k^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1},\dots,u_{k^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},\dots,v_{k^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},\dots,v_{k^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Then, we add all arcs of the perfect matching <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\{u_{i}v_{i}:i\in[1,k^{2}]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\{u_{i}v_{i}:i\in[1,k^{2}]\}</annotation></semantics></math> and all arcs in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{v_{j}u_{i}:i,j\in[1,k^{2}],i\neq j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{j}u_{i}:i,j\in[1,k^{2}],i\neq j\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The following sequence of claims imply the lemma.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 11.1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of claim</span>. This follows from the observation that <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(K_{k^{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(K_{k^{2}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which has total dichromatic number <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 A construction of oriented graphs with large acyclic dichromatic number ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
  <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lozenge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">◊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lozenge</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 11.2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow\vec{C_{3}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow\vec{C_{3}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of claim</span>. Note that, in <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, removing the arcs of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> yields an acyclic oriented graph. Therefore, every directed triangle contains at least one arc of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now assume for a contradiction that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> contains two disjoint directed triangles <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and all arcs from <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(C_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(C_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. By the remark above, there exists <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i\neq j\leqslant k^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>⩽</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i\neq j\leqslant k^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{i},v_{i}\}\subseteq V(C_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{i},v_{i}\}\subseteq V(C_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{j},v_{j}\}\subseteq V(C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{j},v_{j}\}\subseteq V(C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> contains the arc <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j}u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j}u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.
  <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lozenge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">◊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lozenge</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 11.3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(\vec{C_{3}},1,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(\vec{C_{3}},1,1))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of claim</span>. Let us fix a directed triangle <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Again, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has to use an arc of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, so let us denote, without loss of generality, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=(u_{i},v_{i},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=(u_{i},v_{i},x)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[1,k^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[1,k^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> and some <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in N^{+}(v_{i})\cap N^{-}(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in N^{+}(v_{i})\cap N^{-}(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of vertices dominating <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(C)</annotation></semantics></math>, that is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{-}=N^{-}(u_{i})\cap N^{-}(v_{i})\cap N^{-}(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{-}=N^{-}(u_{i})\cap N^{-}(v_{i})\cap N^{-}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Similarly, let <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of vertices being dominated by <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(C)</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from the definition of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and the fact that <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{-}\subseteq N^{-}(u_{i})\cap N^{-}(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{-}\subseteq N^{-}(u_{i})\cap N^{-}(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{-}\subseteq\{v_{j}:j\in[1,i-1]\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{-}\subseteq\{v_{j}:j\in[1,i-1]\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">and that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}\subseteq\{u_{j}:j\in[i+1,k^{2}]\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}\subseteq\{u_{j}:j\in[i+1,k^{2}]\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.4" class="ltx_p">so indeed <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> dominates <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, implying that <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain any copy of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\vec{C_{3}},1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\vec{C_{3}},1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
  <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lozenge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">◊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lozenge</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 11.4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(2,2,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(2,2,1))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem11.Thmclaim4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of claim</span>. Observe first that, by construction, <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the following property:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.Ex6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S3.Ex6.m4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant k^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>⩽</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant k^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, both arcs <math id="S3.Ex6.m4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}u_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}u_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Ex6.m4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}v_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}v_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> belong to exactly one directed triangle.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(<math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\star" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋆</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\star</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Now assume for a contradiction that <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(2,2,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(2,2,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, so there exist pairwise disjoint sets of vertices <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x\},Y=\{y_{1},y_{2}\},Z=\{z_{1},z_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\},Y=\{y_{1},y_{2}\},Z=\{z_{1},z_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> dominates <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> dominates <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> dominates <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Observe that every arc in <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{xy_{1},xy_{2},z_{1}x,z_{2}x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{xy_{1},xy_{2},z_{1}x,z_{2}x\}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to two distinct triangles. By (<a href="#S3.Ex6" title="Equation 
                  
                    ⋆ ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">
                  
                    ⋆
                  
                </span> ‣ <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), it follows that each of these arcs belongs to the bipartite digraph <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[U,V]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[U,V]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\{u_{1},\dots,u_{k^{2}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\{u_{1},\dots,u_{k^{2}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{k^{2}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{k^{2}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">In particular, <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a vertex with in-degree and out-degree at least <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[U,V]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[U,V]</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a contradiction, as every vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math> has out-degree <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[U,V]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[U,V]</annotation></semantics></math>, and every vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> has in-degree <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[U,V]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[U,V]</annotation></semantics></math>. The claim follows.
  <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lozenge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">◊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lozenge</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The lemma follows from the combination of the four previous claims.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 6. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove the statement by induction on the order of <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, the result being trivial when <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(H)|\leqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(H)|\leqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic hero of order at least <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a hero, and by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Berger et al. []). ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> one of the following holds:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">there exist two smaller heroes <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1},H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1},H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}\Rightarrow H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}\Rightarrow H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, or</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">there exist a smaller hero <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and an integer <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(H^{\prime},k,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(H^{\prime},k,1)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(H^{\prime},1,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(H^{\prime},1,k)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">In the first case, since <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are subtournaments of <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, they are both acyclic heroes, and by induction they both belong to <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{AH}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{AH}</annotation></semantics></math>. If both of them contain <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{C_{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{C_{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow\vec{C_{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow\vec{C_{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction to Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 11. ‣ 3.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>. Therefore, there exists <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that one of <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1},H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1},H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TT_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TT_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and the other one is isomorphic to a subtournament of <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}=TT_{k}\Rightarrow(\Delta(k,1,1)\Rightarrow TT_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}=TT_{k}\Rightarrow(\Delta(k,1,1)\Rightarrow TT_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, hence implying that <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a subtournament of <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">In the second case, if <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{C_{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{C_{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\vec{C_{3}},1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\vec{C_{3}},1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction to Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 11. ‣ 3.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>. It follows that <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TT_{k^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TT_{k^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}=|V(H^{\prime})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}=|V(H^{\prime})|</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min(k,k^{\prime})\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min(k,k^{\prime})\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(2,2,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(2,2,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction to Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 11. ‣ 3.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>. Therefore, <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min(k,k^{\prime})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min(k,k^{\prime})=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\max(k,k^{\prime}),1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\max(k,k^{\prime}),1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The result follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> relies on the particular structure of prime heroes. Given a tournament <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, a <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">homogeneous set</span> is a set of vertices <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for every vertex <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in V(T)\setminus X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in V(T)\setminus X</annotation></semantics></math>, either <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq N^{-}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq N^{-}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq N^{+}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq N^{+}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>. The set <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is further <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">nontrivial</span> if <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leqslant|X|\leqslant|V(T)|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leqslant|X|\leqslant|V(T)|-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
A tournament is <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">prime</span> if it does not contain any nontrivial homogeneous set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For an odd integer <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as the tournament with vertex set <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(R_{n})=\{v_{0},\dots,v_{n-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(R_{n})=\{v_{0},\dots,v_{n-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and arc set</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(R_{n})=\left\{v_{i}v_{i+j}:0\leqslant i\leqslant n-1\mbox{ and }1\leqslant j\leqslant\tfrac{n-1}{2}\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(R_{n})=\left\{v_{i}v_{i+j}:0\leqslant i\leqslant n-1\mbox{ and }1\leqslant j\leqslant\tfrac{n-1}{2}\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where indices are taken modulo <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Liu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, Theorem 3.8]</cite> obtained the following result, from which we derive Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 12</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Liu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a prime tournament. If <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=|V(T)|\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=|V(T)|\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove the stronger result that every tournament <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the sake of contradiction, let <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a minimum counterexample, so <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T^{\prime})\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T^{\prime})\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> for every tournament <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> of smaller order. By minimality, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly connected, otherwise we can <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-colour each strongly connected component independently.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We further claim that <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> must be prime. Otherwise, let <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-trivial homogeneous set. If <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> contains a directed triangle, since <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is connected then <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> contains a vertex <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq N^{-}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq N^{-}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> contains a subtournament isomorphic to <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction. Hence, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[X]</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the tournament obtained from <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by contracting <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> into a single vertex <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. By minimality of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> admits an acyclic <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>. It is straightforward to check that <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> extends from <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by giving colour <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(x^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(x^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math> to all the vertices in <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Hence, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is prime, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(T)|\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(T)|\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. By Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12 (Liu []). ‣ 3.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=|V(T)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=|V(T)|</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is straightforward to check that giving vertices <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0},\dots,v_{(n-1)/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0},\dots,v_{(n-1)/2}</annotation></semantics></math> colour <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and vertices <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{(n+1)/2},\dots,v_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{(n+1)/2},\dots,v_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> colour <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> yields an acyclic <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence yielding the contradiction.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We finally show that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(k,1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(k,1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic hero. We first show that it is an acyclic hero in the class of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolourable tournaments, and then combine it with the fact that it is a hero to obtain the result.
We make use of the following celebrated result, obtained in 1964 by Erdős and Moser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 13</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every integer <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, every tournament of order at least <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TT_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TT_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 14</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(k,1,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(k,1,1))</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}(T)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}(T)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2\cdot 4^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2\cdot 4^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove by induction that, for any tournament <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(k,1,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(k,1,1))</annotation></semantics></math> and any partition of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(T)</annotation></semantics></math> into two sets <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{1},V_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{1},V_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[V_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[V_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[V_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[V_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> being acyclic, there exists an acyclic dicolouring of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> using at most <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> colours on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and at most <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> colours on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and that these two sets of colours are disjoint.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Observe first that if one of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty, then the result is trivial, as we may colour every vertex of the other set with the same colour.
Therefore, assume that <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}\neq\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}\neq\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}\neq\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}\neq\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the sinks of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[V_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[V_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[V_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[V_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. We assume by symmetry that the arc between <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> goes from <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}=T-t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}=T-t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and assume by induction that <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic dicolouring of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(V_{1}\setminus\{t_{1}\})\subseteq\{1,\dots,4^{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(V_{1}\setminus\{t_{1}\})\subseteq\{1,\dots,4^{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(V_{2})\subseteq\{4^{k}+1,\dots,2\cdot 4^{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(V_{2})\subseteq\{4^{k}+1,\dots,2\cdot 4^{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> can be extended to <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by choosing for <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> a colour from <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,4^{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,4^{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> that is not given by <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> to any vertex in</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{u\in N^{+}(t_{1})}N^{+}(u)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{u\in N^{+}(t_{1})}N^{+}(u).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume first that such a colour exists, and let us briefly justify that the obtained extension of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is indeed an acyclic dicolouring. If this is not the case, then there exists a directed cycle <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> which is either monochromatic or alternating between two colour classes. Among all such cycles, we let <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be a shortest one, so it has length either <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> (as <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a tournament). By the induction hypothesis, <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, since <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[V_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[V_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic, <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> contains at least one vertex of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and because vertices in <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> use distinct sets of colours, <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is necessarily a directed <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},x,y,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},x,y,z)</annotation></semantics></math> alternating between two colour classes. We conclude that <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(t_{1})=\varphi(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(t_{1})=\varphi(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction, since <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\bigcup_{u\in N^{+}(t_{1})}N^{+}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\bigcup_{u\in N^{+}(t_{1})}N^{+}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now argue that such a colour exists. Recall that <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{+}(t_{1})\subseteq V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{+}(t_{1})\subseteq V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{+}(t_{1})\subseteq N^{-}(t_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{+}(t_{1})\subseteq N^{-}(t_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are sinks in <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[V_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[V_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[V_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[V_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Therefore, we have <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{+}(t_{1})\leqslant 2^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{+}(t_{1})\leqslant 2^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, for otherwise, by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 13. ‣ 3.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[N^{+}(t_{1})\cap N^{-}(t_{2})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[N^{+}(t_{1})\cap N^{-}(t_{2})]</annotation></semantics></math> contains a copy of <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TT_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TT_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting the assumption that <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(k,1,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(k,1,1))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, let us fix a vertex <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in N^{+}(t_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in N^{+}(t_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The same argument, relying on the fact that <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sink in <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[V_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[V_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, shows that <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|N^{+}(u)\cap V_{1}|\leqslant 2^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|N^{+}(u)\cap V_{1}|\leqslant 2^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{u\in N^{+}(t_{1})}N^{+}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{u\in N^{+}(t_{1})}N^{+}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> contains at most <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{2k-2}\leqslant 4^{k}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{2k-2}\leqslant 4^{k}-1</annotation></semantics></math> vertices of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since vertices of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> use colours distinct from <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,4^{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,4^{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that one colour is available for <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, showing the result.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 15</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a hero such that, for some <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, every <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-free tournament <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}(T)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}(T)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant c</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic hero.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-free tournament, and let us show that it has bounded acyclic dichromatic number.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">As <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a hero, there exists <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}(T)\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}(T)\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>. We fix <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},\dots,V_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},\dots,V_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> a partition of <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(T)</annotation></semantics></math> into transitive tournaments. For every <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> be an acyclic dicolouring of <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[V_{i}\cup V_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[V_{i}\cup V_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> using at most <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> colours, which exists by assumption.
For every <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[1,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[1,k]</annotation></semantics></math> and every vertex <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we let</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(u)=\Big(\varphi_{1,i}(u),\varphi_{2,i}(u),\dots,\varphi_{i-1,i}(u),0,\varphi_{i,i+1}(u),\dots,\varphi_{i,k}(u)\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(u)=\Big(\varphi_{1,i}(u),\varphi_{2,i}(u),\dots,\varphi_{i-1,i}(u),0,\varphi_{i,i+1}(u),\dots,\varphi_{i,k}(u)\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">For any vertex <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and integer <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[1,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[1,k]</annotation></semantics></math>, we further denote by <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\rm th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>th</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\rm th}</annotation></semantics></math> coordinate of <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(u)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is a colouring of <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> using at most <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c+1)^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c+1)^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> colours (since every coordinate of <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is an integer of <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,c]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,c]</annotation></semantics></math>). It remains to show that it is indeed an acyclic dicolouring.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume for a contradiction that <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, coloured with <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, contains a directed cycle <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> which is either monochromatic or alternating between two colour classes of <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>. Among all such cycles, we choose <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of minimum length.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note that, by definition, <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}(u)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}(u)=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, each colour class of <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is included in some <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be monochromatic, so it is alternating between <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for some indices <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> has length <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us denote <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}=(u,x,v,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}=(u,x,v,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in V_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in V_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> alternates between two colour classes of <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, in particular we have <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}(u)=\psi_{j}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}(u)=\psi_{j}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, which by definition of <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i,j}(u)=\varphi_{i,j}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i,j}(u)=\varphi_{i,j}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, we have <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i,j}(x)=\varphi_{i,j}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i,j}(x)=\varphi_{i,j}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a directed cycle in <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[V_{i}\cup V_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[V_{i}\cup V_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> alternating between two colour classes of <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction. The result follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> follows directly from the combination of Lemmas <a href="#Thmtheorem14" title="Lemma 14. ‣ 3.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem15" title="Lemma 15. ‣ 3.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Complexity</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we study the complexity of the following problem, defined for every fixed integer <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">

<span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" style="width:331.2pt;">
<span id="S4.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="S4.p2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S4.p2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S4.p2.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Acyclic <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-Dicolourability</span></span></span>
</span>
</span>
<span id="S4.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> 
An oriented graph <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
<span id="S4.p2.1.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question:</span> 
Does <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> admit an acyclic <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring?</span>
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that, by <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 A construction of oriented graphs with large acyclic dichromatic number ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, for any graph <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, one can construct in polynomial time the oriented graph <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
<math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D(G))=\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D(G))=\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall furthermore that <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is a bipartite digraph.
Since, for every integer <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>, it is NP-complete to decide whether a graph is <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-colourable, we directly obtain the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 16</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every integer <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Acyclic <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-Dicolourability</span> is NP-complete even when restricted to bipartite digraphs.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Complexity for split digraphs ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we give another reduction showing that <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Acyclic <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-Dicolourability</span> is NP-hard, for every integer <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>, even on the class of split digraphs. In Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we provide a polynomial-time algorithm for <span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Acyclic <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-Dicolourability</span> on tournaments.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Complexity for split digraphs</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A digraph <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=(V,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=(V,A)</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">split digraph</span> if we can partition its vertices into two sets <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[V_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[V_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> has no arcs and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[V_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[V_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> is a semicomplete digraph. We call <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> a <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">split partition of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(D)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> A split digraph <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=(V,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=(V,A)</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">semicomplete</span> if every vertex in <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to every vertex in <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 17</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every fixed <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Acyclic <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-Dicolouring</span> is NP-complete even when restricted to split digraphs.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the complexity of the problem remains open when <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> or when the input is a complete split digraph.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 18</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What is the complexity of deciding whether a split digraph <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=2</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 19</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What is the complexity of deciding whether a semicomplete split digraph <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=2</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a semicomplete split digraph is a special case of a semicomplete multipartite digraph in which all but one partite set has size one. Hence, proving the above polynomial would be a step towards determining the complexity for multipartite tournaments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this section is devoted to the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 4.1 Complexity for split digraphs ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>. We first prove the hardness for <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 20</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Acyclic <math id="Thmtheorem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-Dicolouring<span id="Thmtheorem20.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is NP-hard even when restricted to split digraphs.</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We reduce from <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">-Dicolourability</span>, which is well-known to be NP-hard (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>). Let <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be any instance of this problem.
We assume that <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> has minimum in-degree at least <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, for otherwise we repeatedly remove sources from <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain an equivalent instance.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We build <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.
To every vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(D)</annotation></semantics></math> we associate a vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, and to every arc <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in A(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in A(D)</annotation></semantics></math> we associate a vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, for every arc <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=uv\in A(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=uv\in A(D)</annotation></semantics></math>, we add the arcs <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{u}x_{a},x_{a}x_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{u}x_{a},x_{a}x_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. We fix an arbitrary ordering <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\dots,a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\dots,a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(D)</annotation></semantics></math>, and add to <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> all the arcs <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{a_{i}}x_{a_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{a_{i}}x_{a_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&gt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&gt;i</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{A}=\{x_{a}:a\in A(D)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{A}=\{x_{a}:a\in A(D)\}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a transitive tournament. We further add a new vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> dominating <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and being dominated by <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{V}=\{x_{v}:v\in V(D)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{V}=\{x_{v}:v\in V(D)\}</annotation></semantics></math>
We finally add the six vertices in
<math id="S4.SS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\{y_{1},y_{2},y_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\{y_{1},y_{2},y_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\{z_{1},z_{2},z_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\{z_{1},z_{2},z_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, together with the arcs in</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{y_{1}y_{2},y_{2}y_{3},y_{3}y_{1}\}\cup\{z_{1}z_{2},z_{2}z_{3},z_{3}z_{1}\}\cup\{x_{a}y,yz,zx_{a}:a\in A(D)\cup\{b\},y\in Y,z\in Z\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{1}y_{2},y_{2}y_{3},y_{3}y_{1}\}\cup\{z_{1}z_{2},z_{2}z_{3},z_{3}z_{1}\}\cup\{x_{a}y,yz,zx_{a}:a\in A(D)\cup\{b\},y\in Y,z\in Z\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">see Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4.1 Complexity for split digraphs ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for an illustration.</span></p>
</div>
