<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>A digraph <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> together with one of its <math id="S4.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolourings <math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> (left), and the corresponding digraph <math id="S4.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> with one of its acyclic <math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolourings (right).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">By construction, <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is an independent set, and <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\star}-X_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\star}-X_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is a tournament, so <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is a split digraph. We now show that <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}(D)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}(D)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D^{\star})\leqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D^{\star})\leqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>, implying the lemma.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 20.1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}(D)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}(D)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D^{\star})\leqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D^{\star})\leqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of claim</span>. Assume that <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon V(D)\to\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon V(D)\to\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\star}\colon V(D^{\star})\to\{1,2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\star}\colon V(D^{\star})\to\{1,2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the colouring of <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\star}(x)=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}\varphi(v)&amp;\mbox{if }x=x_{v}\mbox{ with }v\in V(D)\\
3&amp;\mbox{if }x\in X_{A}\cup\{x_{b}\}\\
1&amp;\mbox{if }x\in\{y_{1},z_{1}\}\\
2&amp;\mbox{otherwise, that is if }x\in\{y_{2},y_{3},z_{2},z_{3}\}.\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> with </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>3</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>otherwise, that is if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\star}(x)=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}\varphi(v)&amp;\mbox{if }x=x_{v}\mbox{ with }v\in V(D)\\
3&amp;\mbox{if }x\in X_{A}\cup\{x_{b}\}\\
1&amp;\mbox{if }x\in\{y_{1},z_{1}\}\\
2&amp;\mbox{otherwise, that is if }x\in\{y_{2},y_{3},z_{2},z_{3}\}.\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">We claim that <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic dicolouring of <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly, each colour class induces an acyclic digraph, and the digraph <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[V_{1},V_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[V_{1},V_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic as well (where <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of vertices coloured <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>). Therefore, if <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is not an acyclic dicolouring, there exists a directed cycle <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> alternating between <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain any vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>, as every in-neighbour of <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> is coloured with <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain any vertex in <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and it does not contain <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is included in the digraph <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[X_{V},X_{A}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[X_{V},X_{A}]</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form</p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}=(x_{u_{1}},x_{u_{1}u_{2}},x_{u_{2}},\ldots,x_{u_{\ell-1}u_{\ell}},x_{u_{\ell}},x_{u_{\ell}u_{1}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}=(x_{u_{1}},x_{u_{1}u_{2}},x_{u_{2}},\ldots,x_{u_{\ell-1}u_{\ell}},x_{u_{\ell}},x_{u_{\ell}u_{1}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.3" class="ltx_p">But then <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}^{\prime}=(u_{1},u_{2},\dots,u_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}^{\prime}=(u_{1},u_{2},\dots,u_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> is a monochromatic directed cycle in <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> coloured with <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.
  <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lozenge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">◊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lozenge</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 20.2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D^{\star})\leqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D^{\star})\leqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}(D)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}(D)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of claim</span>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> be an acyclic <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with colour classes <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},V_{2},V_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},V_{2},V_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and assume without loss of generality that <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{b}\in V_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{b}\in V_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first show that <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{A}\cup\{x_{b}\}\subseteq V_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{A}\cup\{x_{b}\}\subseteq V_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that this is not the case, and assume by symmetry that <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{a}\in V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{a}\in V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in A(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in A(D)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since both <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> induce directed triangles, note that some colour appears on both <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. If colour <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(Y)\cap\varphi(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(Y)\cap\varphi(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{b},y,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{b},y,z)</annotation></semantics></math> is a monochromatic directed triangle for some <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, because of <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> being coloured <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, colour <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> does not belong to <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(Y)\cap\varphi(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(Y)\cap\varphi(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(Y)\cap\varphi(Z)=\{2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(Y)\cap\varphi(Z)=\{2\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If there exists <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(y)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(y)=3</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in Z</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{a},y,z,x_{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{a},y,z,x_{b})</annotation></semantics></math> alternates between <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction. Therefore, <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(Y)=\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(Y)=\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(Z)=\{2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(Z)=\{2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume without loss of generality that <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(y_{1})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(y_{1})=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z_{1})=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z_{1})=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z_{2})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z_{2})=2</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{b},y_{1},z_{1},z_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{b},y_{1},z_{1},z_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> alternates between <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction. This shows <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{A}\cup\{x_{b}\}\subseteq V_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{A}\cup\{x_{b}\}\subseteq V_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{3}\cap X_{V}=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{3}\cap X_{V}=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, if some vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{v}\in X_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{v}\in X_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is coloured <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any entering arc <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> (recall that <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> has minimum in-degree at least <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>), then the 3-cycle <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{b},x_{a},x_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{b},x_{a},x_{v})</annotation></semantics></math> is monochromatic, a contradiction. This allows us to define <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon V(D)\to\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon V(D)\to\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring of <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> where, for every <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(D)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(v)=\varphi^{\star}(x_{v})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(v)=\varphi^{\star}(x_{v}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">We claim that <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, if <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}=(u_{1},u_{2},\dots,u_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}=(u_{1},u_{2},\dots,u_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> is a monochromatic directed cycle in <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> coloured with <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}^{\star}=(x_{u_{1}},x_{u_{1}u_{2}},x_{u_{2}},\ldots,x_{u_{\ell-1}u_{\ell}},x_{u_{\ell}},x_{u_{\ell}u_{1}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}^{\star}=(x_{u_{1}},x_{u_{1}u_{2}},x_{u_{2}},\ldots,x_{u_{\ell-1}u_{\ell}},x_{u_{\ell}},x_{u_{\ell}u_{1}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p11.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p11.3.1" class="ltx_text">is a directed cycle in <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> alternating between two colour classes of <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.
  <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lozenge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">◊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lozenge</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">The lemma follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We now derive <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 4.1 Complexity for split digraphs ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 4.1 Complexity for split digraphs ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">It is clear that <span id="S4.SS1.p14.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Acyclic <math id="S4.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-Dicolourability</span> is in NP. We prove by induction on <math id="S4.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math> that it is NP-hard for every <math id="S4.SS1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>, even when restricted to split digraphs, the case <math id="S4.SS1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding exactly to <a href="#Thmtheorem20" title="Lemma 20. ‣ 4.1 Complexity for split digraphs ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be any digraph and <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a tournament with acyclic dichromatic number <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, whose size depends only on <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> (</span><span id="S4.SS1.p15.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span><span id="S4.SS1.p15.1.3" class="ltx_text"> the tournament constructed in the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 2. ‣ 2 A construction of oriented graphs with large acyclic dichromatic number ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, which has <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}\geqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}\geqslant k</annotation></semantics></math> so contains a subtournament, <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T_{k})=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T_{k})=k</annotation></semantics></math>).
We let <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the digraph obtained by taking disjoint copies <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with one extra vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and putting all arcs in</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{vu,ut,tv:u\in V(D_{k}),t\in V(T_{k})\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{vu,ut,tv:u\in V(D_{k}),t\in V(T_{k})\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p15.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p15.2.1" class="ltx_text">Note that the size of <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is at most a polynomial in the size of <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS1.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> being fixed), and that if <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a split digraph then so is <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The hardness of <span id="S4.SS1.p15.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Acyclic <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-Dicolourability</span> for split digraphs thus follows from the hardness of <span id="S4.SS1.p15.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Acyclic <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-Dicolourability</span> for split digraphs together with the following claim. Note that we may assume that <math id="S4.SS1.p15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly connected, as when computing the acyclic dicolourability of a digraph, it suffices to do so for each of the strong components (and take the maximum over all strong components).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 20.3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D_{k})\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D_{k})\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="Thmtheorem20.Thmclaim3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D_{k+1})\leqslant k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D_{k+1})\leqslant k+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of claim</span>. Assume first that <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D_{k})\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D_{k})\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring of <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by taking an acyclic <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, an acyclic <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and using colour <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume for a contradiction that <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is not an acyclic dicolouring of <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a directed cycle <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> which is either monochromatic or alternating between two colours. Then <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is the only vertex coloured <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is included in a strongly connected component of <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k+1}-v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k+1}-v</annotation></semantics></math>, so in particular <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is included either in <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> or in <math id="S4.SS1.p16.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume now that <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D_{k+1})\leqslant k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D_{k+1})\leqslant k+1</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> be an acyclic <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume without loss of generality that <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is coloured <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>. If both <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> use colour <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a monochromatic directed triangle, a contradiction. Therefore, one of them does not use colour <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> does not use colour <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> uses at most <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> colours on <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and the result follows, so we assume that <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)\notin\psi(V(T_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)\notin\psi(V(T_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T_{k})=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T_{k})=k</annotation></semantics></math>, for every <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[1,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[1,k]</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> coloured <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of vertices coloured <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Clearly, we may assume that <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> has at least one entering arc in <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, for otherwise <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> would not be strongly connected.
Hence, let <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy\in A(D_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy\in A(D_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> be such that <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x)\neq k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x)\neq k+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(y)=k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(y)=k+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Now there exists <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in V(T_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in V(T_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(z)=\psi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(z)=\psi(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,x,y,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,x,y,z)</annotation></semantics></math> is a directed cycle alternating between colour classes <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.
  <math id="S4.SS1.p17.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lozenge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">◊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lozenge</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">The result follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>A polynomial-time algorithm for <span id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Acyclic <math id="S4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-Dicolouring</span> on tournaments</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We proved in the last sections that deciding <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> is NP-hard for general oriented graphs <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. We now show that the situation is different for tournaments. This also draws a distinction with the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">-Dicolourability</span> problem, which is well-known to be NP-hard even when restricted to tournaments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Theorem 2.8.21]</cite> for a shorter proof).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our proof is inspired by a proof due to Klingelhoefer and Newman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> that a <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of a tournament <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed in polynomial time when <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is guaranteed to have dichromatic number <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. Precisely, we make use of a decomposition of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolourable light tournaments that they obtained. A tournament <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">light</span> if, for every arc <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in A(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in A(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, the subtournament <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[N^{+}(v)\cap N^{-}(u)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[N^{+}(v)\cap N^{-}(u)]</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 21</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a polynomial-time algorithm for deciding whether a given tournament <math id="Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> admits an acyclic <math id="Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring, and computing one when it exists.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The cases of more than two colours remain open. We believe that <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Acyclic <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-Dicolouring</span>, when restricted to tournaments, is actually solvable in polynomial time for every fixed <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. We pose the following stronger conjecture. A tournament <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-critical</span> if <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)=k</annotation></semantics></math> and every proper subtournament <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(H)\leqslant k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(H)\leqslant k-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 22</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a finite number of <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-critical tournaments.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If the conjecture above is true, then in particular there is a trivial polynomial time algorithm for deciding <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>, consisting of checking whether <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> contains one of the <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-critical tournaments. The conjecture remains open for every <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this subsection is devoted to the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem21" title="Theorem 21. ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem21" title="Theorem 21. ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be any tournament on <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math> vertices (the algorithm being trivial when <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>). Let us describe the claimed algorithm. Clearly, we may assume that <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly connected, for otherwise we apply the following algorithm to each strongly connected component of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. We first justify that we can further assume that <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is light.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 0.1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is light.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of claim</span>. Assume that <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> be an acyclic <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume for a contradiction that <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is not light, so there exists an arc <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in A(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in A(T)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[N^{+}(v)\cap N^{-}(u)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[N^{+}(v)\cap N^{-}(u)]</annotation></semantics></math> contains a directed triangle <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,z)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume first that <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u)=\varphi(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u)=\varphi(v)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,z)</annotation></semantics></math> is a directed triangle, one of these three vertices, say <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, uses colour <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,x,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,x,u)</annotation></semantics></math> is a monochromatic directed triangle, a contradiction.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume now, without loss of generality, that <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(v)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(v)=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> uses both colours <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x,y,z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x,y,z\}</annotation></semantics></math> (otherwise <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,z)</annotation></semantics></math> is monochromatic), there exists an arc, say <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(y)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(y)=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,x,y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,x,y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> is a directed cycle alternating between colours <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction to <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> being an acyclic dicolouring.
  <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lozenge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">◊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lozenge</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Clearly, one can check in polynomial time whether <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is light. If it is not, then by <a href="#Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim1" title="Claim 0.1. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Claim</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.1</span></a> we have <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math> and we can stop here.
From now on, we thus assume that <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is light.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">An ordered pair <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math> of distinct vertices is <span id="S4.SS2.p11.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">nice</span> if there exists an acyclic <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(s)=\varphi(t)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(s)=\varphi(t)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and, for every <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in N^{+}(s)\cup N^{-}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in N^{+}(s)\cup N^{-}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u)=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a colouring <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is called an <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p11.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-colouring</span> of <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 0.2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if and only if <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> admits a nice pair <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of claim</span>. By definition, if <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> admits a nice pair then it admits an acyclic <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring, and in particular <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, assume that <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> be an acyclic <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the vertices coloured respectively <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, and assume without loss of generality that <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V_{1}|\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V_{1}|\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[V_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[V_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive, let <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> be its sink and <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> be its source. By definition, <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(s)=\varphi(t)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(s)=\varphi(t)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{+}(s)\cup N^{-}(t)\subseteq V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{+}(s)\cup N^{-}(t)\subseteq V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence showing that <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring.
  <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lozenge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">◊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lozenge</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">From now on, let us fix two arbitrary distinct vertices <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,t</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the remaining of the proof, we show that we can decide in polynomial time whether <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a nice pair, and computing an <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring when it is.
Since there exist at most <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> candidates <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math> for being a nice pair, the result then follows from <a href="#Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim2" title="Claim 0.2. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Claim</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first compute a shortest directed <math id="S4.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>-path <math id="S4.SS2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(v_{0},v_{1},\dots,v_{\ell-1},v_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(v_{0},v_{1},\dots,v_{\ell-1},v_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=v_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=v_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=v_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=v_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> (recall that <math id="S4.SS2.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly connected). We sequentially build a sequence <math id="S4.SS2.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},\dots,X_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},\dots,X_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> of disjoint subsets of <math id="S4.SS2.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(T)</annotation></semantics></math> where:</span></p>
