</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of claim</span>. Items <a href="#S4.I1.i1" title="Item (i) ‣ Claim 0.3. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(i)</span></a> and <a href="#S4.I1.i2" title="Item (ii) ‣ Claim 0.3. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(ii)</span></a> follow easily from the definitions.
Item <a href="#S4.I1.i3" title="Item (iii) ‣ Claim 0.3. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a> follows from <a href="#S4.I1.i2" title="Item (ii) ‣ Claim 0.3. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(ii)</span></a> and our assumption that <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is light.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <a href="#S4.I1.i4" title="Item (iv) ‣ Claim 0.3. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iv)</span></a>, observe that the sets <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V(P),X_{0},\dots,X_{\ell+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V(P),X_{0},\dots,X_{\ell+1})</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise disjoint by definition. To see that <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(P)\cup X_{0}\cup\dots\cup X_{\ell+1}=V(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(P)\cup X_{0}\cup\dots\cup X_{\ell+1}=V(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> be any vertex in <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(T)\setminus V(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(T)\setminus V(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> does not have any in-neighbour in <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(P)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is an in-neighbour of <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it belongs to <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Else if it has at least one out-neighbour <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(P)</annotation></semantics></math> then, by definition of <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{j\leqslant i}X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{j\leqslant i}X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The claim follows.
  <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lozenge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">◊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lozenge</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Informally, from now on, our goal is to show that we can divide <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> into smaller pieces <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1},\dots,Z_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1},\dots,Z_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> that are easy to colour, and to show that these pieces can be coloured (almost) independently. We then conclude with a dynamic programming argument.
For this, let us define:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=X_{0}\cup{v_{0}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=X_{0}\cup{v_{0}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=X_{\ell+1}\cup\{v_{\ell}\},\text{~~and}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext> and</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=X_{\ell+1}\cup\{v_{\ell}\},\text{~~and}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=X_{i}\cup\{v_{i-1},v_{i}\}\text{~~for every $i\in[1,\ell]$.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> for every </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=X_{i}\cup\{v_{i-1},v_{i}\}\text{~~for every $i\in[1,\ell]$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p18.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p18.2.1" class="ltx_text">Now each set <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> should correspond to the union of <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> consecutive sets <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>s, unless the number of such sets is too small. Formally, let <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\max(0,\ell-5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\max(0,\ell-5)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&lt;5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&lt;5</annotation></semantics></math>, let</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}=Z_{k}=\bigcup_{i=0}^{\ell+1}Y_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}=Z_{k}=\bigcup_{i=0}^{\ell+1}Y_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p18.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p18.3.1" class="ltx_text">Else if <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geqslant 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geqslant 5</annotation></semantics></math>, for every <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[0,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[0,k]</annotation></semantics></math>, let</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}=\bigcup_{j=i}^{i+6}Y_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}=\bigcup_{j=i}^{i+6}Y_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p18.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p18.4.1" class="ltx_text">In both cases, observe that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i=0}^{k}Z_{i}=\bigcup_{i=0}^{\ell+1}Y_{i}=V(P)\cup\bigcup_{i=0}^{\ell+1}X_{i}=V(T)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i=0}^{k}Z_{i}=\bigcup_{i=0}^{\ell+1}Y_{i}=V(P)\cup\bigcup_{i=0}^{\ell+1}X_{i}=V(T),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p18.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p18.5.1" class="ltx_text">where the last equality follows from Claim <a href="#Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim3" title="Claim 0.3. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.3</span></a><a href="#S4.I1.i4" title="Item (iv) ‣ Claim 0.3. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iv)</span></a>.
Given an index <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[0,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[0,k]</annotation></semantics></math>, a <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\colon Z_{i}\to\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\colon Z_{i}\to\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[Z_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[Z_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p18.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-coherent</span> if:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic dicolouring of <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[Z_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[Z_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in Z_{i}\cap\{s,t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in Z_{i}\cap\{s,t\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u)=1</annotation></semantics></math>; and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in Z_{i}\cap(N^{+}(s)\cup N^{-}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in Z_{i}\cap(N^{+}(s)\cup N^{-}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u)=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Observe that, for any <math id="S4.SS2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring <math id="S4.SS2.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, its restriction to any set <math id="S4.SS2.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-coherent. The first key point is that the converse is also true, hence implying that we only have to find a colouring of <math id="S4.SS2.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(T)</annotation></semantics></math> whose restriction to <math id="S4.SS2.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-coherent for every <math id="S4.SS2.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[0,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[0,k]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 0.4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A colouring <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring if and only if, for every <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[0,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[0,k]</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction of <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-coherent.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p20" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of claim</span>. The “only if” direction is straightforward.
For the opposite direction, assume for a contradiction that <math id="S4.SS2.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is a colouring of <math id="S4.SS2.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for every <math id="S4.SS2.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[0,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[0,k]</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction <math id="S4.SS2.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-coherent, but <math id="S4.SS2.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is not an <math id="S4.SS2.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(T)=\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(T)=\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, in particular each vertex <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\{s,t\}\cup N^{+}(s)\cup N^{-}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\{s,t\}\cup N^{+}(s)\cup N^{-}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to some set <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> being <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-coherent, we have <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(s)=\varphi(t)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(s)=\varphi(t)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and, for every <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in N^{+}(s)\cup N^{-}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in N^{+}(s)\cup N^{-}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u)=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, since <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is not an <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring, <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, coloured with <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, contains a directed cycle which is either monochromatic or alternating between two colour classes. Among all such cycles, we let <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be one of minimum length. Since <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a tournament, <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> has length either <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> (if <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is monochromatic) or <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> (if <math id="S4.SS2.p21.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is alternating).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now observe that <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(\mathscr{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\mathscr{C})</annotation></semantics></math> is not included in any set <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, for otherwise <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> would not be an acyclic dicolouring of <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[Z_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[Z_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction to <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> being <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-coherent. Therefore, there exist two indices <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math> and two vertices <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V(\mathscr{C})\cap Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V(\mathscr{C})\cap Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in V(\mathscr{C})\cap Y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in V(\mathscr{C})\cap Y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geqslant i+7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geqslant i+7</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X_{i}\cup\{v_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X_{i}\cup\{v_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, for otherwise <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=v_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=v_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that we can let <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}=i-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}=i-1</annotation></semantics></math> play the role of <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, we assume that <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in X_{j}\cup\{v_{j-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in X_{j}\cup\{v_{j-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, for otherwise <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=v_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=v_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}=j+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}=j+1</annotation></semantics></math> may play the role of <math id="S4.SS2.p22.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> has length at most <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists in <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math> (and thus in <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>) a directed path <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> of length at most <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Claims <a href="#Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim3" title="Claim 0.3. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.3</span></a><a href="#S4.I1.i1" title="Item (i) ‣ Claim 0.3. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(i)</span></a> and <a href="#S4.I1.i2" title="Item (ii) ‣ Claim 0.3. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(ii)</span></a>, since <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X_{i}\cup\{v_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X_{i}\cup\{v_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in X_{j}\cup\{v_{j-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in X_{j}\cup\{v_{j-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\{v_{i}\}\cup N^{+}(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\{v_{i}\}\cup N^{+}(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{v_{j-1}\}\cup N^{-}(v_{j-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{v_{j-1}\}\cup N^{-}(v_{j-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, by adding at most two arcs, <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> can be extended into a directed path <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j-1}</annotation></semantics></math> of length at most <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the distance from <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j-1}</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j-1-i\geqslant 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j-1-i\geqslant 6</annotation></semantics></math>, this is a contradiction to the choice of <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
The claim follows.
  <math id="S4.SS2.p23.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lozenge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">◊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lozenge</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">The second key point is that, for every <math id="S4.SS2.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[0,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[0,k]</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of <math id="S4.SS2.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-coherent colourings of <math id="S4.SS2.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[Z_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[Z_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> is limited.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 0.5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[0,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[0,k]</annotation></semantics></math>, there is at most <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{8}\cdot(n+1)^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{8}\cdot(n+1)^{7}</annotation></semantics></math> distinct <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-coherent colourings of <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[Z_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[Z_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, the set <math id="Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of such colourings can be computed in polynomial time.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p25" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of claim</span>. Let us fix <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[0,k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[0,k]</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains at most <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> vertices in <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us fix <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, one of the <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{8}</annotation></semantics></math> possible colourings of <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[Z_{i}\cap V(P)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[Z_{i}\cap V(P)]</annotation></semantics></math>, and evaluate the number of possible extensions of <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for at most <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> different indices <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. We will show that, for each of them, at most <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> colourings of <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[X_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[X_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> may extend <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, hence implying the result. For the second part of the statement, it is straightforward to check from the proof below that, in polynomial time, one can iterate over the <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{7}\cdot(n+1)^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{7}\cdot(n+1)^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> possible colourings, and for each of them check that it is actually <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-coherent.
The structure of possible <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-coherent colourings of <math id="S4.SS2.p25.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[Z_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[Z_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
