<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that, by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 13. ‣ 3.3 Proof of Theorem ‣ 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, every tournament of order <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> contains a transitive subtournament of order <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil\log n\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil\log n\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that every tournament of order <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> admits an acyclic <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-\lceil\log n\rceil+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-\lceil\log n\rceil+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring, which consists of colouring a largest transitive subtournament with one colour, and using one dedicated colour for every other vertex.
We slightly improve on this trivial upper bound by showing the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 23</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem23.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem23.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be any tournament of order <math id="Thmtheorem23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant n-\alpha\cdot\log(n)+27," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant n-\alpha\cdot\log(n)+27,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem23.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem23.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\log^{-1}(4/3)&gt;2.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\log^{-1}(4/3)&gt;2.4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It turns out that, up to the multiplicative constant <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem23" title="Theorem 23. ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> is best possible.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 24</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem24.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem24.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for every integer <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geqslant n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩾</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geqslant n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a tournament <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> with</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant n-\beta\cdot\log(n)-\log\log(n)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant n-\beta\cdot\log(n)-\log\log(n),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem24.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem24.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\frac{8}{\log(8/7)}&lt;60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\frac{8}{\log(8/7)}&lt;60</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 24. ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem23" title="Theorem 23. ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> together raise the following problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 25</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem25.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Find the infimum <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of all real numbers <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> such that there exist infinitely many tournaments <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant n-c\cdot\log(n)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant n-c\cdot\log(n).</annotation></semantics></math>
So far, we have <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.4&lt;\gamma&lt;60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.4</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.4&lt;\gamma&lt;60</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The remaining of this section is devoted to the proofs of Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem23" title="Theorem 23. ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 24. ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem23" title="Theorem 23. ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> relies on the notion of <span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acyclic matchings</span>. A <span id="S5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">matching</span> <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in a tournament <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of pairwise disjoint arcs of <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. It is <span id="S5.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acyclic</span> if, for every pair of arcs <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv,xy\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv,xy\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, the oriented bipartite graph <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[\{u,v,x,y\}]\setminus\{uv,xy\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[\{u,v,x,y\}]\setminus\{uv,xy\}</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem23" title="Theorem 23. ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon x\mapsto\alpha\cdot\log(x)-27(1-\frac{1}{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon x\mapsto\alpha\cdot\log(x)-27(1-\frac{1}{x})</annotation></semantics></math> be defined over <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We prove by induction on <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> that every tournament <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices admits an acyclic matching <math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> of size at least <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The statement then follows, as an acyclic <math id="S5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-|\mathcal{M}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-|\mathcal{M}|)</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained by giving a common colour to any pair of vertices matched in <math id="S5.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a private colour to each of the remaining vertices.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Observe that <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)\leqslant 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)\leqslant 0</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leqslant 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leqslant 11</annotation></semantics></math>, so the result trivially holds in this case. We thus assume that <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geqslant 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geqslant 12</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in A(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in A(T)</annotation></semantics></math> be an arc such that either <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a source or</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|N^{+}(u)\cap N^{-}(v)|\leqslant\frac{n-3}{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|N^{+}(u)\cap N^{-}(v)|\leqslant\frac{n-3}{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">To see that such an arc exists, let <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> be any vertex of <math id="S5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with minimum in-degree, and assume that <math id="S5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}(v)\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}(v)\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math> (otherwise <math id="S5.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vw</annotation></semantics></math> may play the role of <math id="S5.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S5.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in N^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in N^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>). Since <math id="S5.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in V(T)}d^{-}(v)=\binom{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in V(T)}d^{-}(v)=\binom{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}(v)\leqslant\frac{n-1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}(v)\leqslant\frac{n-1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in N^{-}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in N^{-}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> be any vertex whose out-degree is minimum in <math id="S5.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[N^{-}(v)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[N^{-}(v)]</annotation></semantics></math>. Again, <math id="S5.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> has out-degree at most <math id="S5.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}(\frac{n-1}{2}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}(\frac{n-1}{2}-1)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[N^{-}(v)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[N^{-}(v)]</annotation></semantics></math>, and it follows that, in <math id="S5.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|N^{+}(u)\cap N^{-}(v)|\leqslant\frac{n-3}{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|N^{+}(u)\cap N^{-}(v)|\leqslant\frac{n-3}{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a source, let <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=N^{+}(u)\cap N^{-}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=N^{+}(u)\cap N^{-}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. Let <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the tournament <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-(X\cup\{u,v\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-(X\cup\{u,v\})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(T^{\prime})|\geqslant\lceil\frac{1}{4}(3n-5)\rceil\geqslant 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(T^{\prime})|\geqslant\lceil\frac{1}{4}(3n-5)\rceil\geqslant 8</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="S5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is increasing over <math id="S5.p8.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[8,+\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>8</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[8,+\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, by induction, <math id="S5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> admits an acyclic matching <math id="S5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of size at least <math id="S5.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\frac{3n-5}{4}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\frac{3n-5}{4}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}^{\prime}\cup\{uv\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}^{\prime}\cup\{uv\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic. To see this, let <math id="S5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy</annotation></semantics></math> be any arc of <math id="S5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we only have to show that <math id="S5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=T[\{u,v,x,y\}]\setminus\{xy,uv\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=T[\{u,v,x,y\}]\setminus\{xy,uv\}</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic. Note that <math id="S5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is an orientation of <math id="S5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, so it is acyclic if and only if it contains a source or a sink. Therefore, if <math id="S5.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in N^{+}(u)\cap N^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in N^{+}(u)\cap N^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in N^{-}(u)\cap N^{-}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in N^{-}(u)\cap N^{-}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic. By construction, of <math id="S5.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we thus have <math id="S5.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in N^{-}(u)\cap N^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in N^{-}(u)\cap N^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> and analogously <math id="S5.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in N^{-}(u)\cap N^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in N^{-}(u)\cap N^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S5.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a sink and <math id="S5.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic, as desired.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to check that <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+f\left(\frac{3n-5}{4}\right)\geqslant f(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+f\left(\frac{3n-5}{4}\right)\geqslant f(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Note first that, for any real <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&gt;5/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&gt;5/4</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\log(x)-\log\left(x-\frac{5}{4}\right)=\log\left(1+\frac{5}{4x-5}\right)\leqslant\frac{5}{(4x-5)\ln(2)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\log(x)-\log\left(x-\frac{5}{4}\right)=\log\left(1+\frac{5}{4x-5}\right)\leqslant\frac{5}{(4x-5)\ln(2)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">where in the last inequality we used that <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln(1+x)\leqslant x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln(1+x)\leqslant x</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(1+x)=\frac{1}{\ln(2)}\ln(1+x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(1+x)=\frac{1}{\ln(2)}\ln(1+x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows that</span></p>
