<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We now prove <a href="#Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 24. ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>. Our proof relies on probabilistic arguments. To minimize the value of <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain, we first evaluate the probabilities that random orientations of specific small graphs are acyclic. For this, we make use of the following well-known result of Stanley <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.
For every graph <math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a unique polynomial <math id="S5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}(G,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}(G,x)</annotation></semantics></math>, called the <span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">chromatic polynomial</span> of <math id="S5.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, with the property that, for every <math id="S5.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of proper <math id="S5.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-colourings of <math id="S5.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly <math id="S5.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{P}(G,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{P}(G,k)</annotation></semantics></math>, see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Section 14.7]</cite>. Stanley’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> draws a connection between the chromatic polynomial of a graph and the number of its acyclic orientations.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 26</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Stanley <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem26.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem26.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem26.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every graph <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> admits <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathscr{P}(G,-1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathscr{P}(G,-1)|</annotation></semantics></math> distinct acyclic orientations.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We derive the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 27</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem27.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem27.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist 14 acyclic orientations of <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2,2}</annotation></semantics></math>, 46 acyclic orientations of <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2,3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and 230 acyclic orientations of <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{3,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{3,3}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, if <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2,2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2,3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{3,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{3,3}</annotation></semantics></math> are random orientations of <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2,2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2,3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{3,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{3,3}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, then</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(D_{2,2}\mbox{\rm~is acyclic})=\frac{7}{8}\mbox{,~~~}\mathbb{P}(D_{2,3}\mbox{\rm~is acyclic})=\frac{23}{32}\mbox{,~~~and~~~}\mathbb{P}(D_{3,3}\mbox{\rm~is acyclic})=\frac{115}{256}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is acyclic</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">, </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is acyclic</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>23</mn><mn>32</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">,   and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is acyclic</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>115</mn><mn>256</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(D_{2,2}\mbox{\rm~is acyclic})=\frac{7}{8}\mbox{,~~~}\mathbb{P}(D_{2,3}\mbox{\rm~is acyclic})=\frac{23}{32}\mbox{,~~~and~~~}\mathbb{P}(D_{3,3}\mbox{\rm~is acyclic})=\frac{115}{256}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Straightforward counting arguments imply:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathscr{P}(K_{2,2},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathscr{P}(K_{2,2},x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
