</table>
<p id="S5.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#Thmtheorem26" title="Theorem 26 (Stanley []). ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>, for integers <math id="S5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,m</annotation></semantics></math>, the number <math id="S5.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> of acyclic orientations of <math id="S5.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely <math id="S5.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathscr{P}(K_{n,m},-1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathscr{P}(K_{n,m},-1)|</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{A}_{2,2}(x)=14,\text{~~}\mathscr{A}_{2,3}(x)=46,\text{~~and~~}\mathscr{A}_{3,3}(x)=230," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>46</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒜</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>230</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{A}_{2,2}(x)=14,\text{~~}\mathscr{A}_{2,3}(x)=46,\text{~~and~~}\mathscr{A}_{3,3}(x)=230,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p13.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text">as desired. The second part of the statement follows from the observation that <math id="S5.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> admits <math id="S5.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{nm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{nm}</annotation></semantics></math> distinct orientations.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p14.1" class="ltx_p">We slightly extend the notion of acyclic matching as follows. An <span id="S5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acyclic partition</span> in a tournament <math id="S5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a collection <math id="S5.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},\dots,X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},\dots,X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> of subsets of <math id="S5.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(T)</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S5.I1.i1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for each <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[t]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leqslant|X_{i}|\leqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leqslant|X_{i}|\leqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S5.I1.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the sets <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1},\dots,X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1},\dots,X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise disjoint; and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S5.I1.i3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant t</annotation></semantics></math>, the oriented bipartite graph <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[X_{i},X_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[X_{i},X_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p15.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a subset <math id="S5.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq V(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq V(T)</annotation></semantics></math> of vertices is <span id="S5.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">partitionable</span> if there exists an acyclic partition <math id="S5.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},\dots,X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},\dots,X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\bigcup_{i=1}^{t}X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\bigcup_{i=1}^{t}X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We now prove Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 24. ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>, which we recall here for convenience.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p16.1" class="ltx_p">See <a href="#Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 24. ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">We do not give the exact value of <math id="S5.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we simply assume that it is large enough so that all upcoming inequalities hold.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a random tournament on <math id="S5.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices (that is, each edge <math id="S5.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u,v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u,v\}</annotation></semantics></math> is oriented independently and uniformly at random). Let <math id="S5.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\beta\cdot\log(n)+\log\log(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\beta\cdot\log(n)+\log\log(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. We first show that, with positive probability, none of the <math id="S5.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-sets <math id="S5.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq V(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq V(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is partitionable. We then conclude that, with positive probability, <math id="S5.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\geqslant n-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\geqslant n-k</annotation></semantics></math>, hence implying the result.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us fix a pairwise disjoint collection <math id="S5.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},\dots,X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},\dots,X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> of sets of size either <math id="S5.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, and whose union has size <math id="S5.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=\lceil k\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=\lceil k\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. We first bound the probability that <math id="S5.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},\dots,X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},\dots,X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic partition in <math id="S5.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S5.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant t</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the event that the bipartite oriented graph <math id="S5.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[X_{i},X_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[X_{i},X_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic. By <a href="#Thmtheorem27" title="Lemma 27. ‣ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>, we have</span></p>
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(E_{i,j})=\frac{7}{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(E_{i,j})=\frac{7}{8}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X_{i}|=|X_{j}|=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X_{i}|=|X_{j}|=2</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(E_{i,j})=\frac{23}{32}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>23</mn><mn>32</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(E_{i,j})=\frac{23}{32}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X_{i}|\neq|X_{j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X_{i}|\neq|X_{j}|</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(E_{i,j})=\frac{115}{256}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>115</mn><mn>256</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(E_{i,j})=\frac{115}{256}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X_{i}|=|X_{j}|=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X_{i}|=|X_{j}|=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.p19.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p19.2.1" class="ltx_text">For any distinct pairs <math id="S5.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}\subseteq[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}\subseteq[k]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r,s\}\subseteq[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r,s\}\subseteq[k]</annotation></semantics></math>, observe that the events <math id="S5.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent.
It follows that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\Big((X_{1},\dots,X_{t})\mbox{ is an acyclic partition}\Big)\leqslant\underbrace{\left(\frac{7}{8}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}k_{2}(k_{2}-1)}\cdot\left(\frac{23}{32}\right)^{k_{2}k_{3}}\cdot\left(\frac{115}{256}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}k_{3}(k_{3}-1)}}_{g(k_{2},k_{3})=}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is an acyclic partition</mtext></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>23</mn><mn>32</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>115</mn><mn>256</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></munder></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\Big((X_{1},\dots,X_{t})\mbox{ is an acyclic partition}\Big)\leqslant\underbrace{\left(\frac{7}{8}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}k_{2}(k_{2}-1)}\cdot\left(\frac{23}{32}\right)^{k_{2}k_{3}}\cdot\left(\frac{115}{256}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}k_{3}(k_{3}-1)}}_{g(k_{2},k_{3})=},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p19.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p19.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S5.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively <math id="S5.p19.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, denotes the number of sets <math id="S5.p19.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size 2, respectively 3 (So <math id="S5.p19.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=2k_{2}+3k_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=2k_{2}+3k_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Let us simplify the upper bound above. Note that <math id="S5.p19.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(k_{2},k_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(k_{2},k_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the right-hand side above.
Let us fix two arbitrary real numbers <math id="S5.p19.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2},\ell_{3}\geqslant 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2},\ell_{3}\geqslant 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p19.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\ell_{2}+3\ell_{3}=\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\ell_{2}+3\ell_{3}=\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S5.p19.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is large enough, we have</span></p>
