</table>
<p id="S5.p19.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p19.4.1" class="ltx_text">where in the last inequality we used that either <math id="S5.p19.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p19.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is large enough.
Since <math id="S5.p19.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k_{2}+3k_{3}=\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k_{2}+3k_{3}=\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows from the remark above and a straightforward recurrence that either <math id="S5.p19.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="S5.p19.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(k_{2},k_{3})\leqslant g(\ell/2,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(k_{2},k_{3})\leqslant g(\ell/2,0)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p19.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="S5.p19.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(k_{2},k_{3})\leqslant g(\frac{\ell-3}{2},1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(k_{2},k_{3})\leqslant g(\frac{\ell-3}{2},1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that</span></p>
