<p id="S5.p19.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p19.12.1" class="ltx_text">By the union bound, we conclude on the existence of a tournament in which none of the sets of size <math id="S5.p19.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p19.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math> is partitionable. We fix such a tournament <math id="S5.p19.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and claim that <math id="S5.p19.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant n-\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant n-\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math>, hence implying the result. Assume for a contradiction that <math id="S5.p19.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant n-\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant n-\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S5.p19.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> be an acyclic dicolouring of <math id="S5.p19.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> using at most <math id="S5.p19.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-\ell</annotation></semantics></math> colours.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},\dots,Y_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},\dots,Y_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be the colour classes of <math id="S5.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>, labelled such that <math id="S5.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Y_{i}|\geqslant|Y_{i+1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Y_{i}|\geqslant|Y_{i+1}|</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S5.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i\leqslant n-\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i\leqslant n-\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S5.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Y_{1}|\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Y_{1}|\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>, otherwise <math id="S5.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> uses <math id="S5.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> colours. Let <math id="S5.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> be the largest index such that <math id="S5.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Y_{i}|\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Y_{i}|\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S5.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\bigcup_{i=1}^{h}Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\bigcup_{i=1}^{h}Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Observe that the number of colours used by <math id="S5.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-\sum_{i=1}^{h}(|Y_{i}|-1)\leqslant n-\ell," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>h</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-\sum_{i=1}^{h}(|Y_{i}|-1)\leqslant n-\ell,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p20.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p20.2.1" class="ltx_text">hence implying <math id="S5.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Y|\geqslant\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Y|\geqslant\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math>. By successively removing from <math id="S5.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> either a single vertex in a colour class of size at least <math id="S5.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> or both vertices in a colour class of size exactly <math id="S5.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude on the existence of <math id="S5.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq Y</annotation></semantics></math> and a partition <math id="S5.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},\dots,X_{h^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},\dots,X_{h^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p20.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</span></p>
<ul id="S5.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\leqslant|X|\leqslant\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\leqslant|X|\leqslant\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[h^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[h^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}\subseteq Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}\subseteq Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[h^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[h^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X_{i}|\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X_{i}|\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.p20.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p20.3.1" class="ltx_text">For every <math id="S5.p20.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[h^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[h^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S5.p20.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{i}^{1},\dots,X_{i}^{\iota_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{i}^{1},\dots,X_{i}^{\iota_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary partition of <math id="S5.p20.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> into sets of size either <math id="S5.p20.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p20.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> (which necessarily exists as <math id="S5.p20.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X_{i}|\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X_{i}|\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>).
We consider the partition</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1}^{1},\dots,X_{1}^{\iota_{1}},X_{2}^{1},\dots,X_{h^{\prime}-1}^{\iota_{h^{\prime}-1}},X_{h^{\prime}}^{1},\dots,X_{h^{\prime}}^{\iota_{h^{\prime}}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ι</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mi>ι</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1}^{1},\dots,X_{1}^{\iota_{1}},X_{2}^{1},\dots,X_{h^{\prime}-1}^{\iota_{h^{\prime}-1}},X_{h^{\prime}}^{1},\dots,X_{h^{\prime}}^{\iota_{h^{\prime}}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p20.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p20.4.1" class="ltx_text">that we relabel <math id="S5.p20.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1},\dots,Z_{\zeta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1},\dots,Z_{\zeta}</annotation></semantics></math> for convenience. We finally argue that <math id="S5.p20.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{i})_{i\in[\zeta]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{i})_{i\in[\zeta]}</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic partition, hence showing that <math id="S5.p20.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is partitionable, a contradiction as <math id="S5.p20.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|\in\{\ell,\ell+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|\in\{\ell,\ell+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">To see this, let <math id="S5.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i&lt;j\leqslant\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>. By construction, there exist <math id="S5.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant i^{\prime},j^{\prime}\leqslant h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⩽</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant i^{\prime},j^{\prime}\leqslant h</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}\subseteq Y_{i^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}\subseteq Y_{i^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{j}\subseteq Z_{j^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{j}\subseteq Z_{j^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}\neq j^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}\neq j^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S5.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[Z_{i},Z_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[Z_{i},Z_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> is a subdigraph of <math id="S5.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[Y_{i^{\prime}},Y_{j^{\prime}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[Y_{i^{\prime}},Y_{j^{\prime}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is acyclic because <math id="S5.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic dicolouring. If <math id="S5.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}=j^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}=j^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[Z_{i},Z_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[Z_{i},Z_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> is a subdigraph of <math id="S5.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[Y_{i^{\prime}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[Y_{i^{\prime}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is acyclic because <math id="S5.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic dicolouring. The result follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Degenerate graphs</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer. A graph <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-degenerate</span> if every non-empty subgraph <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains a vertex of degree at most <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is well-known that <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-degenerate graphs have unbounded acyclic chromatic number (see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, Corollary 3]</cite>), while <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-degenerate graphs (<span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> forests) trivially admit an acyclic <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show a similar behaviour in the directed setting. A digraph is <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-degenerate if its underlying graph is.
Given a digraph <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and a partition <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(D)</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> the digraph <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> after reversing the direction of all arcs between <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will show the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 28</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem28.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem28.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem28.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-degenerate oriented graph <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a partition <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(D)</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We will prove the theorem by induction on the order, <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, of <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly the theorem holds when the order is at most <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, since in this case we can use the partition <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varnothing,V(D))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varnothing,V(D))</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic and <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\{v\},V(D)\setminus\{v\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\{v\},V(D)\setminus\{v\})</annotation></semantics></math> for
any <math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(D)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is not acyclic (and therefore <math id="S6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle). So assume <math id="S6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geqslant 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geqslant 4</annotation></semantics></math> and the theorem
holds for all smaller values of <math id="S6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-degenerate, there exists a vertex <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V(D)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(x)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(x)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}=D-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}=D-x</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be a partition of <math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(D^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(D^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic, which exists by our induction hypothesis.
If <math id="S6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(x)\leqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(x)\leqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math> we can add <math id="S6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to either <math id="S6.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in order to get the desired partition of <math id="S6.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> (as <math id="S6.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> cannot belong to any cycles), so we may assume that <math id="S6.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(x)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(x)=2</annotation></semantics></math>. We now consider the following cases, which exhaust all possibilities.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1.<span id="S6.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{+}(x)=d^{-}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{+}(x)=d^{-}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span><span id="S6.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and both neighbours of </span><math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> belong to the same </span><math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span><span id="S6.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">In this case, we may without loss of generality assume that <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}xv_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}xv_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a path in <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},v_{2}\in X_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},v_{2}\in X_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If there is a <math id="S6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{1},v_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{1},v_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-path, <math id="S6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, in <math id="S6.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> then we will let <math id="S6.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If there is a cycle in <math id="S6.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, then the cycle must use the path <math id="S6.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}xv_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}xv_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. But substituting
<math id="S6.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}xv_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}xv_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the cycle by <math id="S6.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> gives us a closed walk in <math id="S6.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore also a cycle
in <math id="S6.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction. So <math id="S6.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic in this case.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">So we may assume that there is no <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{1},v_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{1},v_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-path, <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, in <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, let
<math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If there now is a cycle in <math id="S6.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, then the cycle must use the path <math id="S6.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}xv_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}xv_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and a path from <math id="S6.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, we assumed there was no <math id="S6.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{1},v_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{1},v_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-path in <math id="S6.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction. So in Case 1 we can always
find the desired partition.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2.<span id="S6.p8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{+}(x)=d^{-}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{+}(x)=d^{-}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span><span id="S6.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and the neighbours of </span><math id="S6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> belong to different sets in </span><math id="S6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span><span id="S6.p8.1.5" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">In this case, let <math id="S6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math> would also work).
Now the out-degree or in-degree of <math id="S6.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> will be zero so <math id="S6.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> will be acyclic.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 3.<span id="S6.p10.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{d^{+}(x),d^{-}(x)\}=\{0,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{d^{+}(x),d^{-}(x)\}=\{0,2\}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span><span id="S6.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and both neighbours of </span><math id="S6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> belong to the same </span><math id="S6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p10.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span><span id="S6.p10.1.5" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">In this case, let <math id="S6.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S6.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math> would also work).
Now the out-degree or in-degree of <math id="S6.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> will be zero so <math id="S6.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> will be acyclic.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 4.<span id="S6.p12.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S6.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{d^{+}(x),d^{-}(x)\}=\{0,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{d^{+}(x),d^{-}(x)\}=\{0,2\}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span><span id="S6.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and the neighbours of </span><math id="S6.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p12.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> belong to different sets in </span><math id="S6.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p12.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span><span id="S6.p12.1.5" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof of this case follows similar lines to that of Case 1.
In this case, assume without loss of generality, and by directional duality, that <math id="S6.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{+}(x)=\{v_{1},v_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{+}(x)=\{v_{1},v_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\in X_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\in X_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">If there is a <math id="S6.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{2},v_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{2},v_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-path, <math id="S6.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, in <math id="S6.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> then we will let <math id="S6.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If there is a cycle in <math id="S6.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, then the cycle must use the path <math id="S6.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}xv_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}xv_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. But substituting
<math id="S6.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}xv_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}xv_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the cycle by <math id="S6.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> gives us a closed walk in <math id="S6.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore also a cycle
in <math id="S6.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction. So <math id="S6.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic in this case.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">So we may assume that there is no <math id="S6.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{2},v_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{2},v_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-path in <math id="S6.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, let
<math id="S6.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=X_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}=X_{2}^{\prime}\cup\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If there now is a cycle in <math id="S6.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, then the cycle must use the path <math id="S6.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}xv_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}xv_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and a path from <math id="S6.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, we assumed there was no <math id="S6.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{2},v_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{2},v_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-path in <math id="S6.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{\prime})^{rev}(X_{1}^{\prime},X_{2}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction. So in Case 4 we can always
find the desired partition, and the result follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 29</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem29.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem29.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem29.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-degenerate oriented graph satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem28" title="Theorem 28. ‣ 6 Degenerate graphs ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>, we note that
there exists a partition <math id="S6.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(D)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic.
However, this implies that <math id="S6.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[X_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[X_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[X_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[X_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[X_{1},X_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[X_{1},X_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> must all be acyclic as well.
So <math id="S6.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired colouring.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p17.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, we derive the following, as outerplanar graphs are <math id="S6.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-degenerate (see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Exercise 10.2.11]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem30" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem30.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 30</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem30.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem30.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem30.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every oriented outerplanar graph <math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p18.1" class="ltx_p">We now show that Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem29" title="Corollary 29. ‣ 6 Degenerate graphs ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a> is best possible by constructing <math id="S6.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-degenerate oriented graphs with arbitrarily large acyclic dichromatic number. We make use of the celebrated result of Ramsey in the following general form, which can be found in many basic textbooks on graph theory, see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Theorem 9.1.3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem31" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 31</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem31.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="Thmtheorem31.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Ramsey</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem31.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem31.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem31.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem31.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,b,r\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,b,r\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an integer <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geqslant r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geqslant r</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for every mapping <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon\binom{[n]}{b}\to[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon\binom{[n]}{b}\to[k]</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\binom{[n]}{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\binom{[n]}{r}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(x)=\varphi(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(x)=\varphi(y)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\binom{X}{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\binom{X}{b}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p19.1" class="ltx_p">For integers <math id="S6.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,b,r\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,b,r\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S6.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}(b,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}(b,r)</annotation></semantics></math> the smallest integer <math id="S6.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> for which Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem31" title="Theorem 31 (Ramsey []). ‣ 6 Degenerate graphs ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a> holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem32" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem32.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 32</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem32.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem32.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem32.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem32.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-degenerate oriented graph <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\geqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\geqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us fix an arbitrary integer <math id="S6.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> and build a <math id="S6.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-degenerate oriented graph <math id="S6.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\geqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\geqslant k</annotation></semantics></math>. We let <math id="S6.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be the oriented graph obtained as follows. Start with an independent set <math id="S6.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S6.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)\cdot R_{k-1}(3,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)\cdot R_{k-1}(3,4)</annotation></semantics></math>, and take an arbitrary acyclic order <math id="S6.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prec" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≺</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prec</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. For every triplet <math id="S6.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u,v,w\}\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u,v,w\}\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\prec v\prec w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≺</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>≺</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\prec v\prec w</annotation></semantics></math>, we add a vertex <math id="S6.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{u,v,w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{u,v,w}</annotation></semantics></math> and the arcs <math id="S6.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{u,v,w}u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{u,v,w}u</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="S6.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{u,v,w}w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{u,v,w}w</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vx_{u,v,w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vx_{u,v,w}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Clearly, <math id="S6.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-degenerate since every vertex <math id="S6.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V(D)\setminus S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V(D)\setminus S</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S6.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is an independent set. Assume for a contradiction that <math id="S6.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant k-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S6.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> be an acyclic <math id="S6.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S6.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. By the pigeonhole principle, there exists a colour <math id="S6.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k-1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}=|S\cap\varphi^{-1}(i)|\geqslant R_{k-1}(3,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}=|S\cap\varphi^{-1}(i)|\geqslant R_{k-1}(3,4)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">We define <math id="S6.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\colon\binom{S^{\prime}}{3}\to[k-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\colon\binom{S^{\prime}}{3}\to[k-1]</annotation></semantics></math> as follows: for every triplet <math id="S6.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u,v,w\}\subseteq S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u,v,w\}\subseteq S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\prec v\prec w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≺</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>≺</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\prec v\prec w</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S6.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(\{u,v,w\})=\varphi(x_{u,v,w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(\{u,v,w\})=\varphi(x_{u,v,w})</annotation></semantics></math>. By Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem31" title="Theorem 31 (Ramsey []). ‣ 6 Degenerate graphs ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a> and by definition of <math id="S6.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k-1}(3,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k-1}(3,4)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S6.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime\prime}=\{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3},s_{4}\}\subseteq S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime\prime}=\{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3},s_{4}\}\subseteq S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is constant on <math id="S6.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{S^{\prime}}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{S^{\prime}}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume without loss of generality that <math id="S6.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}\prec s_{2}\prec s_{3}\prec s_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≺</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≺</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≺</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}\prec s_{2}\prec s_{3}\prec s_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=(s_{2},x_{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}},s_{3},x_{s_{2},s_{3},s_{4}},s_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=(s_{2},x_{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}},s_{3},x_{s_{2},s_{3},s_{4}},s_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is a directed <math id="S6.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle of <math id="S6.p22.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p22.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(s_{2})=\varphi(s_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(s_{2})=\varphi(s_{4})</annotation></semantics></math> (because <math id="S6.p22.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2},s_{4}\in S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2},s_{4}\in S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S6.p22.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(x_{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}})=\varphi(x_{s_{2},s_{3},s_{4}})=\psi(\{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(x_{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}})=\varphi(x_{s_{2},s_{3},s_{4}})=\psi(\{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}\})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, <math id="S6.p22.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is either monochromatic or alternating between two colours, a contradiction to the choice of <math id="S6.p22.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Remarks and further open problems</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude with a branch of further open problems. We start with the case of planar graphs. In 1979, Borodin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> obtained the following celebrated result, resolving a conjecture of Grünbaum <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem33" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem33.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 33</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem33.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Borodin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem33.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem33.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem33.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem33.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every planar graph admits a proper <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-colouring such that the union of any two colour classes induces a forest.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The same bound directly follows for the acyclic dichromatic number.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem34" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem34.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 34</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem34.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem34.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem34.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem34.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every planar oriented graph <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem35" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem35.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 35</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem35.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem35.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem35.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem35.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a planar oriented graph <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the digraph <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from a directed triangle <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v,w)</annotation></semantics></math> by further gluing <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W[u,v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W[u,v]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W[v,w]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W[v,w]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W[w,u]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W[w,u]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W[x,y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W[x,y]</annotation></semantics></math> is the planar oriented graph given in Figure <a href="#S7.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 7 Remarks and further open problems ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. The statement follows from the observation that, in every <math id="S7.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S7.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W[x,y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W[x,y]</annotation></semantics></math>, both <math id="S7.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> receive the same colour.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume for a contradiction that <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic <math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring of <math id="S7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W[x,y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W[x,y]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(y)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(y)=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot receive the same colour, for otherwise either <math id="S7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z_{1},x,z_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z_{1},x,z_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z_{1},y,z_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z_{1},y,z_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a monochromatic directed cycle. Moreover, we have <math id="S7.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z_{1})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z_{1})=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z_{2})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z_{2})=2</annotation></semantics></math>, for otherwise <math id="S7.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,z_{2},z_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,z_{2},z_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is a directed cycle alternating between colours <math id="S7.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S7.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z_{3})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z_{3})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, otherwise <math id="S7.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y,z_{2},z_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y,z_{2},z_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> is monochromatic.
By symmetry, we obtain that <math id="S7.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z_{6})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z_{6})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z_{5})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z_{5})=2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z_{4})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z_{4})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="S7.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,z_{4},z_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,z_{4},z_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> is monochromatic, a contradiction.
∎</span></p>
