atrix(-0.5145 0.85748 -0.85748 -0.5145 104.64 -16.91)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 6.48 0 C 4.56 0.36 1.44 1.44 -0.72 2.7 L -0.72 -2.7 C 1.44 -1.44 4.56 -0.36 6.48 0"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 159.01 107.54 L 104.64 16.91"></path><g transform="matrix(-0.5145 -0.85748 0.85748 -0.5145 104.64 16.91)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 6.48 0 C 4.56 0.36 1.44 1.44 -0.72 2.7 L -0.72 -2.7 C 1.44 -1.44 4.56 -0.36 6.48 0"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 171.32 -108.17 L 226.07 -16.91"></path><g transform="matrix(0.5145 0.85748 -0.85748 0.5145 226.07 -16.91)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 6.48 0 C 4.56 0.36 1.44 1.44 -0.72 2.7 L -0.72 -2.7 C 1.44 -1.44 4.56 -0.36 6.48 0"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 171.69 107.54 L 226.07 16.91"></path><g transform="matrix(0.5145 -0.85748 0.85748 0.5145 226.07 16.91)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 6.48 0 C 4.56 0.36 1.44 1.44 -0.72 2.7 L -0.72 -2.7 C 1.44 -1.44 4.56 -0.36 6.48 0"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 167.63 -106.74 L 185.11 -19.34"></path><g transform="matrix(0.19612 0.98058 -0.98058 0.19612 185.11 -19.34)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 6.48 0 C 4.56 0.36 1.44 1.44 -0.72 2.7 L -0.72 -2.7 C 1.44 -1.44 4.56 -0.36 6.48 0"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 186.38 12.99 L 169.04 99.68"></path><g transform="matrix(-0.19612 0.98058 -0.98058 -0.19612 169.04 99.68)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 6.48 0 C 4.56 0.36 1.44 1.44 -0.72 2.7 L -0.72 -2.7 C 1.44 -1.44 4.56 -0.36 6.48 0"></path></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>The digraph <math id="S7.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W[x,y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W[x,y]</annotation></semantics></math> together with one of its acyclic <math id="S7.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolourings.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We do not believe that the bound of Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem34" title="Corollary 34. ‣ 7 Remarks and further open problems ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a> is tight and pose the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem36" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem36.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 36</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem36.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem36.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem36.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem36.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every oriented planar graph <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude with several complexity problems. The first one was already mentioned in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem37" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem37.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 37</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem37.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem37.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem37.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem37.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every fixed <math id="Thmtheorem37.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math> it is polynomial to decide whether a tournament <math id="Thmtheorem37.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="Thmtheorem37.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We proved in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> that there exists a polynomial-time algorithm deciding whether a tournament has an acyclic <math id="S7.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dicolouring. We wonder if such an algorithm can be extended to cover larger classes of oriented graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem38" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem38.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 38</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem38.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem38.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem38.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What is the complexity, for every fixed <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, of deciding if a digraph <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(D)\leqslant k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(D)\leqslant k</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Already the case when <math id="S7.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(D)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(D)=2</annotation></semantics></math> is open, even for digraphs whose vertex set can be covered by two semicomplete digraphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p9.1" class="ltx_p">A digraph <math id="S7.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S7.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">locally tournament digraph</span> if <math id="S7.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[N^{+}[v]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[N^{+}[v]]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D[N^{-}[v]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D[N^{-}[v]]</annotation></semantics></math> are tournaments for every vertex <math id="S7.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. These digraphs, introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, form a rich generalization of tournaments and share many properties with these <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Chapter 6]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem39" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem39.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 39</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem39.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem39.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem39.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What is the complexity of deciding <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a locally tournament digraph?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the definition of light still works for locally tournament digraphs and the proof of Claim <a href="#Thmtheorem0.Thmclaim1" title="Claim 0.1. ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.1</span></a> carries over to locally tournament digraphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Another generalization of tournaments is the class of <span id="S7.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">multipartite tournaments</span> which are orientations of complete multipartite graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem40" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem40.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 40</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem40.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem40.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem40.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What is the complexity of deciding whether a multipartite tournament has acyclic dichromatic number at most 2?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p12.1" class="ltx_p">This is open already for bipartite tournaments, but polynomial for the special class of multipartite tournaments called <span id="S7.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">extended tournaments</span>. These oriented graphs are of the form <math id="S7.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=T[I_{k_{1}},I_{k_{2}},\ldots{},I_{k_{t}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=T[I_{k_{1}},I_{k_{2}},\ldots{},I_{k_{t}}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S7.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a tournament and <math id="S7.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from <math id="S7.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by substituting an independent set of vertices <math id="S7.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S7.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th vertex of <math id="S7.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[|V(T)|]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[|V(T)|]</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to check that if <math id="S7.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is an extension of <math id="S7.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S7.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Another generalization of tournaments is the class of <span id="S7.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">quasi-transitive digraphs</span>. These are digraphs containing no induced directed path of length 2. They have a very useful recursive structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem41" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem41.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 41</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem41.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem41.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem41.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="Thmtheorem41.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a><span id="Thmtheorem41.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="Thmtheorem41.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">

Let <math id="Thmtheorem41.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be a quasi-transitive digraph.</span></p>
<ul id="S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S7.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is not strongly connected, then there exists a transitive oriented graph </span><math id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=T[H_{1},H_{2},\ldots{},H_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=T[H_{1},H_{2},\ldots{},H_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where each </span><math id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a strongly connected quasi-transitive digraph.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is strongly connected, then there exists a strong semicomplete digraph </span><math id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that
</span><math id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=S[Q_{1},Q_{2},\ldots{},Q_{s}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=S[Q_{1},Q_{2},\ldots{},Q_{s}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> where each </span><math id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is either a single vertex or a non-strong quasi-transitive digraph.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a strong quasi-transitive digraph <math id="S7.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=S[Q_{1},Q_{2},\ldots{},Q_{s}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=S[Q_{1},Q_{2},\ldots{},Q_{s}]</annotation></semantics></math> contains the semicomplete digraph <math id="S7.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> as an induced subdigraph (just take an arbitrary vertex from each <math id="S7.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>). Thus, <math id="S7.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> only if we also have <math id="S7.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(S)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(S)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>. The other direction does not always hold, e.g. in the case when each <math id="S7.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a directed cycle. In fact, the following holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem42" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem42.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 42</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem42.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem42.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem42.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem42.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A strong quasi-transitive digraph <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=S[Q_{1},Q_{2},\ldots{},Q_{s}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=S[Q_{1},Q_{2},\ldots{},Q_{s}]</annotation></semantics></math>
has <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=2</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(S)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(S)=2</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S7.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=S[Q_{1},Q_{2},\ldots{},Q_{s}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=S[Q_{1},Q_{2},\ldots{},Q_{s}]</annotation></semantics></math> be a strong quasi-transitive digraph without 2-cycles. By the remark above, we may assume that <math id="S7.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(S)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(S)=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose first that some <math id="S7.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a cycle <math id="S7.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S7.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> be an acyclic 2-dicolouring of <math id="S7.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S7.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> contains an arc <math id="S7.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(u)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(u)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(v)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(v)=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S7.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is strong and hence vertex pancyclic by Moon’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Theorem 1.5.1]</cite>, there are indices <math id="S7.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,k</annotation></semantics></math> distinct from <math id="S7.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> contains the subdigraph <math id="S7.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{C}_{3}[Q_{i},Q_{j},Q_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{C}_{3}[Q_{i},Q_{j},Q_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math>. If both of <math id="S7.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{j},Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{j},Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> contain a vertex coloured <math id="S7.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S7.p15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S7.p15.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> contains a monochromatic triangle so we may assume that <math id="S7.p15.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> uses one colour <math id="S7.p15.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> on all vertices of <math id="S7.p15.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and the other colour <math id="S7.p15.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\neq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\neq a</annotation></semantics></math> on all vertices of <math id="S7.p15.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining this with the fact that <math id="S7.p15.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> uses both colours of <math id="S7.p15.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain a contradiction to <math id="S7.p15.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> being an acyclic 2-dicolouring of <math id="S7.p15.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume next that each <math id="S7.p15.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic. Fix one vertex <math id="S7.p15.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in each <math id="S7.p15.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and recall that these vertices induce a copy of <math id="S7.p15.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.p15.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Now let <math id="S7.p15.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> be an acyclic 2-dicolouring of (this copy of) <math id="S7.p15.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we can extend <math id="S7.p15.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> to an acyclic 2-dicolouring of <math id="S7.p15.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> by giving all vertices of <math id="S7.p15.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the colour <math id="S7.p15.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(s_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(s_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that above we can extend any acyclic 2-dicolouring of <math id="S7.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> to all of <math id="S7.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> when each <math id="S7.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic so we obtain the following corollary of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem21" title="Theorem 21. ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem43" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem43.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 43</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem43.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem43.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem43.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem43.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a polynomial-time algorithm for checking whether a quasi-transitive digraph <math id="Thmtheorem43.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="Thmtheorem43.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p17.1" class="ltx_p">The concept of acyclic dichromatic number only makes sense for oriented graphs but, due to Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem21" title="Theorem 21. ‣ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2-Dicolouring on tournaments ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>, the following problem, which in the language of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> can be seen as an orientation completion problem, may be of interest. Here we think of a mixed graph <math id="S7.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(V,E\cup{}A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(V,E\cup{}A)</annotation></semantics></math> as corresponding to the digraph <math id="S7.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=(V,A^{*}\cup A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=(V,A^{*}\cup A)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S7.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from <math id="S7.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing every edge in <math id="S7.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> by a directed 2-cycle. Equivalently, we are looking for a spanning oriented subgraph of a digraph <math id="S7.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> that we obtain by deleting precisely one arc from each 2-cycle of <math id="S7.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem44" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem44.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 44</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem44.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem44.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem44.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Is there a polynomial-time algorithm for deciding whether a semicomplete digraph <math id="Thmtheorem44.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=(V,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=(V,A)</annotation></semantics></math> contains a spanning tournament <math id="Thmtheorem44.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(V,A^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(V,A^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem44.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p18.1" class="ltx_p">It follows from Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem16" title="Corollary 16. ‣ 4 Complexity ‣ Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> that it is NP-complete to decide whether an oriented bipartite graph has acyclic dichromatic number 2. Hence, in general, it is NP-complete to decide whether a bipartite digraph contains a spanning oriented subdigraph with acyclic chromatic number at most 2. However, if the oriented part of a bipartite digraph <math id="S7.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic, or every arc is in a 2-cycle, then it is easy to see that <math id="S7.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> always has a spanning oriented subdigraph with acyclic dichromatic number at most 2. This motivates the following problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem45" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem45.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 45</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem45.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem45.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem45.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classify the class of bipartite digraphs for which it is polynomial to decide the existence of a spanning oriented subdigraph with acyclic dichromatic number at most 2.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
